{"v":2,"lessons":[{"id":"ko:c0","title":"@c_pron","level":"@beginner","kind":"grammar","intro":"Korean vowels can be short or long, but this is not indicated in writing and the distinction rarely if ever affects meaning. (example: 밤 bam, pronounced short means \"night\", pronounced long means \"chestnut\")","sections":[{"heading":"Vowels","body":"a ㅏ : like 'a' in \"father\" · o ㅗ : like 'o' in \"tone\" · eo (ŏ) ㅓ : like the \"uh\" in \"lust\" · u ㅜ : A low sound of \"oo\" as in \"hoop\". \"woo\" (Korean does not distinguish between \"oo\" and \"woo\"). · eu (ŭ) ㅡ : like 'i' in \"cousin\", \"dozen\". Like the Turkish \"ı\", Polish \"y\" or Russian \"ы\". Kind of similar to the French \"eu\", but as a clearer, purer vowel sound. · i ㅣ : like the 'i' in \"ship\" (short) OR the 'ee' in \"sheep\" (long) · e ㅔ : like the 'e' in \"bed\" · ae ㅐ : similar to the \"a\" in \"hand\", \"valve\", \"gas\", and \"can\""},{"heading":"Common diphthongs","body":"oe ㅚ : like 'we' in 'west' (it used to be a different sound, now pronounced the same as ㅞ shown below) · ui ㅢ : like 'ŭ' + 'i' · wa ㅘ : like 'wa' sound in \"suave\" · wae ㅙ : like 'wa' in \"wagon\". Some would argue there is virtually no difference anymore from ㅞ. · wo ㅝ : like 'wuh' sound in \"wonder\" · wi ㅟ : like \"we\" or 'e' in \"she\" with rounded lips · we ㅞ : like 'we' in \"west\" · ya ㅑ : like 'ya' in \"yard\" · yo ㅛ : like 'yo' in \"yosemite\" or \"New York\". Not like \"yaw\" or \"yoke\". · yeo (yŏ) ㅕ : like 'you' in \"young\" · yu ㅠ : like \"you\" · ye ㅖ : like 'ye' in \"yes\" · yae ㅒ : like 'ye' in \"yes\"; it has virtually assimiliated to be the same as 'ㅖ'"},{"heading":"Consonants","body":"b (p) ㅂ : like 'p' in \"spit\" (unaspirated) · p (p', ph) ㅍ : like 'p' in \"pig\" (aspirated) · pp ㅃ : tensed 'p', like 'p' in \"petit\" in French · d (t) ㄷ : like 't' in \"stab\" (unaspirated) · t (t', th) ㅌ : like 't' in \"top\" (aspirated) · tt ㄸ : tensed 't' · g (k) ㄱ : like 'k' in \"skate\" (unaspirated) · k (k', k) ㅋ : like 'c' in \"cat\" (aspirated) · kk ㄲ : tensed 'k' · j (ch) ㅈ : like 'g' in \"gin\" (unaspirated) · ch (ch') ㅊ : like 'ch' in \"chin\" (aspirated). Usually pronounced as a light aspirated 't' as a final consonant · jj ㅉ : tensed 'j' · s ㅅ : like 's' in \"soon\", 'sh' before i or any \"y\" diphthong. Usually pronounced as a very light 't' as a final consonant · ss ㅆ : tensed 's', 's' in 'sea', never 'sh' · n ㄴ : like 'n' in \"nice\" · m ㅁ : like 'm' in \"mother\" · l ㄹ : somewhere between 'l', 'r' and 'n', original sound is 'r' or 'l'. and 'n' sound occurs through initial consonant mutation. · h ㅎ : like 'h' in \"help\" · ng ㅇ : like 'ng' in \"sing\". Unpronounced (placeholder) when at the start of a syllable."}],"phrases":[]},{"id":"ko:c1","title":"@c_grammar","level":"@beginner","kind":"grammar","intro":"Korean sentence structure is very similar to that of Japanese, so speakers of Japanese will find many aspects of Korean grammar familiar, and vice versa. But there are similar but slight differences to the standardized pronunciations, and the Korean language, even after its simplification in the past century, has a wider library of vowels and consonants than Japanese, hence Japanese speakers may find it difficult to pronounce various words, let alone transcribe them.","sections":[{"heading":"1","body":"Korean word order is subject-object-verb: \"I-subject him-object see-verb.\" Subjects (especially I and you) are often omitted if these are clear from the context. This may seem awkward from an English perspective, but English too has colloquial 1st-person/2nd-person subject omissions, such as \"[Are you] Done yet?\" or \"[I'm] Done.\" It is a matter of whether sentences are common enough that such lack of subjects doesn't confuse the listener. In turn, some English colloquial sentences without subjects may be confusing from a Korean standpoint."},{"heading":"2","body":"There are no articles, genders, or declensions. It has extensive verb conjugations indicating tense and honorific level. There is a handy, universal plural form, but it is very often omitted."},{"heading":"3","body":"Korean has postpositions instead of prepositions: jip mite, \"house below\" instead of \"below the house.\""},{"heading":"4","body":"Koreans refer to each other rather in terms like elder brother, elder sister, younger sibling, uncle, aunt, grandmother, grandfather, manager, teacher etc. (like Nepali or Chinese) than by using the word you. Additionally, it's not uncommon to refer to yourself by using such an expression ( example: \"[I] Father will cook you a nice dinner.\" Which feels like saying \"This father will...\"). You can also call somebody an aunt, uncle or brother even if this person is actually not. Many Korean girls call even their boyfriend \"oppa\" (older brother)."},{"heading":"5","body":"Keep in mind that the words for older brother and older sister differ depending on the gender of the subject. The word for older brother is 형 (hyeong) if the subject is male, and 오빠 (oppa) if the subject is female, while the word for older sister is 누나 (nuna) if the subject is male, and 언니 (eonni) if the subject is female."}],"phrases":[]},{"id":"ko:c2","title":"@c_first","level":"@beginner","intro":"@c_intro","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"안녕하십니까. Common in North Korea, Yanbian, provincial South Korea.","f":"Hello. (formal)","r":"annyeonghasimnikka"},{"t":"안녕하세요. Common in South Korea. to older people or to the people to meet first","f":"Hello.","r":"annyeonghaseyo"},{"t":"안녕. to your friend or younger people","f":"Hello. (informal)","r":"annyeong"},{"t":"여보세요. when you answer the phone.","f":"Hello. (on the phone)","r":"yeoboseyo"},{"t":"어떻게 지내십니까?","f":"How are you?","r":"eotteoke jinaesimnikka?"},{"t":"잘 지냅니다, 감사합니다.","f":"Fine, thank you.","r":"jal jinaemnida, gamsahamnida"},{"t":"성함이 어떻게 되세요?","f":"What is your name?","r":"seonghami eotteoke doeseyo?"},{"t":"제 이름은 ______입니다.","f":"My name is ______ .","r":"je ireumeun ____imnida"},{"t":"만나서 반갑습니다.","f":"Nice to meet you.","r":"mannaseo ban-gapseumnida"},{"t":"부탁합니다.","f":"Please.","r":"butakamnida"},{"t":"감사합니다.","f":"Thank you.","r":"gamsahamnida"},{"t":"천만입니다.","f":"You're welcome.","r":"cheonmanimnida"},{"t":"예/네.","f":"Yes.","r":"ye/ne"},{"t":"아니요.","f":"No.","r":"aniyo"},{"t":"실례합니다.","f":"Excuse me. (getting attention)","r":"sill(y)e hamnida"},{"t":"죄송합니다. or 미안해","f":"I'm sorry.","r":"joesonghamnida"},{"t":"안녕.","f":"Goodbye (informal)","r":"annyeong"},{"t":"여기에 영어를 하시는 분 계십니까?","f":"Is there someone here who speaks English?","r":"yeogie yeong-eoreul hasineun bun gyesimnikka?"},{"t":"천천히 말해 주십시오.","f":"Please speak slowly.","r":"cheoncheonhi malhae jusipsio"},{"t":"다시 한번 말해 주십시오.","f":"Please say it again.","r":"dasi hanbeon malhae jusipsio"},{"t":"저는 {언어를} [잘] 못합니다.","f":"I can't speak {language} [well].","r":"jeoneun {eoneoreul} [jal] motamnida"},{"t":"저는 영어를 [잘] 못합니다.","f":"I can't speak English [well].","r":"jeoneun yeong-eoreul [jal] motamnida"},{"t":"____를 하십니까?","f":"Do you speak {language}?","r":"____reul hasimnikka?"},{"t":"네, 조금만요.","f":"Yes, a little.","r":"ne, jogeummanyo"},{"t":"도와주십시오! , 도와주세요!","f":"Help!","r":"dowajusipsio!"},{"t":"조심하십시오! , 조심하세요!","f":"Look out!","r":"josimhasipsio!"},{"t":"좋은 아침입니다.","f":"Good morning.","r":"jo-eun achimimnida"},{"t":"좋은 저녁입니다.","f":"Good evening.","r":"jo-eun jeonyeogimnida"},{"t":"좋은 밤입니다.","f":"Good night.","r":"jo-eun bamimnida"},{"t":"안녕히 주무십시오.","f":"Good night (to sleep)","r":"annyeonghi jumusipsio"},{"t":"이해가 안 갑니다.","f":"I don't understand.","r":"ihaega an gamnida"},{"t":"화장실이 어디에 있습니까?","f":"Where is the toilet?","r":"hwajangsiri eodie itseumnikka?"},{"t":"무엇입니까?","f":"What?","r":"mueosimnikka?"},{"t":"어디입니까?","f":"Where?","r":"eodiimnikka?"},{"t":"누구입니까?","f":"Who?","r":"nuguimnikka?"},{"t":"언제입니까?","f":"When?","r":"eonjeimnikka?"},{"t":"어느 것입니까?","f":"Which?","r":"eoneu geosimnikka?"},{"t":"얼마나요?","f":"How much?","r":"eolmanayo?"},{"t":"_____은 한국말로 어떻게 말합니까?","f":"How do you say _____ in Korean?","r":"____eun Han-gungmallo eotteoke malhamnikka?"},{"t":"이것은/저것은 무엇이라고 부릅니까?","f":"What is this/that called?","r":"igeoseun/jeogeoseun mueosirago bureumnikka?"}]},{"id":"ko:c3","title":"@c_time","level":"@beginner","intro":"Sino-Korean numbers are used to count minutes and days, while native Korean numbers are used to count hours. Months and years may be counted using either system, depending on which counter is used, while both systems can be used interchangeably to count weeks. However, durations from 1 day to 10 days, as well as 15 days and 30 days, have special words that are commonly used instead of the regular forms stated here.","sections":[{"heading":"@c_remember","body":"The names of the months in Korean are simply the Sino-Korean numbers 1 through 12 followed by the word 월 (wol, meaning \"month\")."},{"heading":"@c_remember","body":"Koreans generally write the date in yyyy.mm.dd format (e.g. 2006.12.25 for December 25th, 2006)."}],"phrases":[{"t":"지금","f":"now","r":"jigeum"},{"t":"나중에","f":"later","r":"najung-e"},{"t":"전에","f":"before","r":"jeone"},{"t":"후에","f":"after","r":"hue"},{"t":"아침","f":"morning","r":"achim"},{"t":"오후","f":"afternoon","r":"ohu"},{"t":"저녁","f":"evening","r":"jeonyeok"},{"t":"밤","f":"night","r":"bam"},{"t":"새벽","f":"dawn","r":"saebyeok"},{"t":"오전 한 시","f":"one o'clock AM","r":"ojeon han si"},{"t":"오전 두 시","f":"two o'clock AM","r":"ojeon du si"},{"t":"정오","f":"noon","r":"jeong-o"},{"t":"오후 한 시","f":"one o'clock PM","r":"ohu han si"},{"t":"오후 두 시","f":"two o'clock PM","r":"ohu du si"},{"t":"자정","f":"midnight","r":"jajeong"},{"t":"_____ 분","f":"_____ minute(s)","r":"___ bun"},{"t":"_____ 시간","f":"_____ hour(s)","r":"___ sigan"},{"t":"_____ 일","f":"_____ day(s)","r":"___ il"},{"t":"_____ 주 / _____ 주일","f":"_____ week(s)","r":"___ ju"},{"t":"오늘","f":"today","r":"oneul"},{"t":"어제","f":"yesterday","r":"eoje"},{"t":"내일","f":"tomorrow","r":"naeil"},{"t":"그저께","f":"the day before yesterday","r":"geujeokke"},{"t":"모레","f":"the day after tomorrow","r":"more"},{"t":"이번 주","f":"this week","r":"ibeon ju"},{"t":"지난 주","f":"last week","r":"jinan ju"},{"t":"다음 주","f":"next week","r":"da-eum ju"},{"t":"1월 irol","f":"January","r":""},{"t":"2월 iwol","f":"February","r":""},{"t":"3월 samwol","f":"March","r":""},{"t":"4월 sawol","f":"April","r":""},{"t":"5월 owol","f":"May","r":""},{"t":"6월 yuwol","f":"June*","r":""},{"t":"7월 chirwol","f":"July","r":""},{"t":"8월 parwol","f":"August","r":""},{"t":"9월 guwol","f":"September","r":""},{"t":"10월 siwol","f":"October*","r":""},{"t":"11월 sibirwol","f":"November","r":""},{"t":"12월 sibiwol","f":"December","r":""}]},{"id":"ko:c4","title":"@c_colours","level":"@beginner","intro":"@c_intro","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"검은색","f":"black","r":"geomeunsaek"},{"t":"흰색","f":"white","r":"huinsaek"},{"t":"회색","f":"gray","r":"hoesaek"},{"t":"빨간색 / 붉은색","f":"red","r":"ppalgansaek"},{"t":"파란색 / 푸른색","f":"blue","r":"paransaek"},{"t":"노란색","f":"yellow","r":"noransaek"},{"t":"초록색","f":"green","r":"choroksaek"},{"t":"주황색","f":"orange","r":"juhwangsaek"},{"t":"자주색","f":"purple","r":"jajusaek"},{"t":"갈색","f":"brown","r":"galsaek"},{"t":"금색","f":"gold","r":"geumsaek"},{"t":"은색","f":"silver","r":"eunsaek"}]},{"id":"ko:c5","title":"@c_problems","level":"@elementary","intro":"@c_intro","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"혼자 내버려 두십시오.","f":"Leave me alone.","r":"honja naebeoryeo dusipsio"},{"t":"만지지 마십시오! , 만지지 마!","f":"Don't touch me!","r":"manjiji masipsio!"},{"t":"경찰을 부르겠습니다!","f":"I'll call the police.","r":"gyeongchareul bureugetseumnida!"},{"t":"경찰!","f":"Police!","r":"gyeongchal!"},{"t":"서라! 도둑이야!","f":"Stop! Thief!","r":"seora! dodugiya!"},{"t":"당신의 도움이 필요합니다.","f":"I need your help.","r":"dangsinui doumi piryohamnida"},{"t":"응급 상황입니다.","f":"It's an emergency.","r":"eunggeup sanghwang-imnida"},{"t":"길을 잃었습니다.","f":"I'm lost.","r":"gireul ireotseumnida"},{"t":"가방을 잃었습니다.","f":"I lost my bag.","r":"gabang-eul ireotseumnida"},{"t":"지갑을 잃었습니다.","f":"I lost my wallet.","r":"jigabeul ireotseumnida"},{"t":"아픕니다.","f":"I'm sick.","r":"apeumnida"},{"t":"상처를 입었습니다.","f":"I've been injured.","r":"sangcheoreul ibeotseumnida"},{"t":"의사가 필요합니다.","f":"I need a doctor.","r":"uisaga piryohamnida"},{"t":"당신의 전화기를 사용해도 되겠습니까?","f":"Can I use your phone?","r":""}]},{"id":"ko:c6","title":"@c_transport","level":"@elementary","intro":"@c_intro","sections":[],"phrases":[{"t":"택시!","f":"Taxi!","r":"taeksi!"},{"t":"_____로 데려가 주십시오. , _____로 가 주세요.","f":"Take me to _____, please.","r":"____ro deryeoga jusipsio"},{"t":"_____까지는 얼마입니까?","f":"How much does it cost to get to _____?","r":"____kkajineun (yogeumi) eolmaimnikka?"},{"t":"저기에 데려가 주십시오.","f":"Take me there, please.","r":"jeogie deryeoga jusipsio'."},{"t":"_____로 가는 표가 얼마입니까?","f":"How much is a ticket to _____?","r":"_____ro ganeun pyoga eolmaimnikka?"},{"t":"_____로 가는 표 한 장이요.","f":"One ticket to _____, please.","r":"_____ro ganeun pyo han jang-iyo"},{"t":"이 기차/버스는 어디로 갑니까?","f":"Where does this train/bus go?","r":"i gicha/beoseu-neun eodiro gamnikka?"},{"t":"_____에 가는 기차/버스는 어디에 있습니까?","f":"Where is the train/bus to _____?","r":"_____e ganeun gicha/beoseuneun eodi-e itseumnikka?"},{"t":"이 기차/버스는 _____에 섭니까?","f":"Does this train/bus stop in _____?","r":"i gicha/beoseu-neun _____e seomnikka?"},{"t":"_____에 가는 기차/버스는 언제 출발합니까?","f":"When does the train/bus for _____ leave?","r":"_____e ganeun gicha/beoseu-neun eonje chulbalhamnikka?"},{"t":"이 기차/버스는 _____에 언제 도착합니까?","f":"When will this train/bus arrive in _____?","r":"i gicha/beoseu-neun _____e eonje dochakamnikka?"},{"t":"_____에 가려면 어떻게 해야 합니까?","f":"How do I get to _____ ?","r":"____e garyeomyeon eotteoke haeya hamnikka?"},{"t":"기차역...?","f":"...the train station?","r":"gichayeok...?"},{"t":"버스 정류장...?","f":"...the bus station?","r":"beoseu jeongnyujang...?"},{"t":"공항...?","f":"...the airport?","r":"gonghang...?"},{"t":"시내...?","f":"...downtown?","r":"sinae...?"},{"t":"유스 호스텔...?","f":"...the youth hostel?","r":"yuseu hoseutel...?"},{"t":"_____ 호텔...?","f":"...the 